In both GvsExtractCallset.wdl and GvsExtractCallsetPgen.wdl, there is a call to
Utils.GetBQTableLastModifiedDatetime as FilterSetInfoTimestamp that fetches the last-modified
timestamp of the filter_set_info table. However, the output of this call
(FilterSetInfoTimestamp.last_modified_timestamp) is never used anywhere in either workflow.
  
The intent appears to have been to bust Cromwell call-caching on the extract task when the
filter_set_info table changes (e.g. after a new filter set is created). The extract task
receives a max_last_modified_timestamp input for this purpose, which is computed by
GetBQTablesMaxLastModifiedTimestamp - but that call only checks the extract table prefixes,
not filter_set_info.

As a result, if a new filter set is created but the extract table prefixes are unchanged,
Cromwell may serve a cached result from a previous extract that used a different filter set.

Confirm the above is the actual intent of FilterSetInfoTimestamp
Determine the correct fix - likely either feeding
FilterSetInfoTimestamp.last_modified_timestamp into GetBQTablesMaxLastModifiedTimestamp
alongside the other timestamps, or passing it directly to the extract task as an additional
cache-busting input, or deleting the call if we really don’t want to use its output. 
Check whether GvsExtractCohortFromSampleNames.wdl has the same gap
